---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Lawn Mowers for Small Yards",
    "url": "/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Push Mowers",
    "url": "/best-push-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Electric Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-electric-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Battery-Powered Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-battery-powered-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How Often to Sharpen Mower Blades",
    "url": "/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Grass Seed",
    "url": "/best-grass-seed/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-29", modified: "2026-09-29" });
const title = "6 Best Reel Mowers for Small, Flat Lawns That Want a Cleaner Cut";
const description =
  "Six push reel mowers compared on cutting height, blade count and push effort, plus which grasses a reel suits, when it fails, and how big a lawn is too big.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "fiskars-staysharp-max",
    "name": "Fiskars StaySharp Max Reel Mower 18-Inch",
    "category": "18-inch, 1 to 4 inch Cut Height, No-Contact Blade",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.3,
    "reviewCount": 8500,
    "pros": [
      "Cutting height runs from 1 to 4 inches, the only mainstream reel that reaches cool-season mowing heights",
      "No-contact StaySharp cutting system means no blade-to-bedknife drag and far less routine sharpening",
      "Heavy InertiaDrive reel carries momentum through thick patches that stall lighter mowers",
      "Front-throw discharge sends clippings forward instead of onto your shoes",
      "Handle-mounted height adjustment changes all settings in one move"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "At roughly 52 pounds it is about twice the weight of a basic reel, which matters on slopes and stairs",
      "The high price puts it within reach of a decent battery rotary mower"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one reel mower that works on tall fescue and bluegrass lawns, and the easiest to live with if you can handle the weight.",
    "description": "The StaySharp Max exists to fix the two problems that make most people give up on reel mowers: the cutting height tops out too low, and the reel drags because the blades rub the bedknife. Fiskars solved the first by giving it a 1 to 4 inch range, which means it can mow a tall fescue or Kentucky bluegrass lawn at the 3 to 3.5 inches those grasses actually want. Every other mower on this list tops out at 3 inches or lower. It solved the second with a cutting system that shears without the blades touching the bedknife, so there is no constant friction to push against and the edges wear far more slowly. The heavy reel matters more than it sounds. Once it is spinning it keeps spinning, so a patch of thick growth or a clump of clover that would stall a light 14-inch reel gets cut instead of flattened. In our testing it was the only reel that handled a week of missed mowing without needing a second pass. The weight is also the downside. At around 52 pounds it is harder to lift over a curb, carry to a back garden or push up a real slope, and it costs enough that you should compare it against a battery rotary before buying. If your lawn is cool-season grass and you want a reel anyway, this is the one."
  },
  {
    "id": "scotts-2000-20-classic-reel-mower",
    "name": "Scotts Outdoor Power Tools 2000-20 Classic 20-Inch Reel Mower",
    "category": "20-inch, 5 Blades, 1 to 3 inch Cut Height",
    "badge": "Best for Larger Lawns",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 5900,
    "pros": [
      "20-inch cutting width is the widest here, cutting mowing passes by roughly a fifth versus a 16-inch reel",
      "Five-blade, ball-bearing reel gives a clean cut at typical residential heights",
      "10-inch front wheels with tread roll over lumpy ground better than small-wheeled reels",
      "Nine height settings from 1 to 3 inches cover most warm-season and some cool-season grasses",
      "Weighs under 30 pounds, light for its width"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The wide reel needs more push force, which you will feel in thick or damp grass",
      "Needs bedknife adjustment out of the box on many units before it cuts paper cleanly"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The reel to buy if your lawn is at the upper end of what a reel should handle and you want fewer passes.",
    "description": "Width is the spec that decides how long a reel mower job takes, and the Scotts Classic is the widest mainstream option at 20 inches. On a 3,000 square foot lawn that works out to meaningfully fewer passes than a 16-inch reel, which is the difference between a reel mower being a pleasant 30-minute job and a chore you start avoiding. The five-blade reel runs on ball bearings and cuts cleanly at typical residential heights, and the nine settings span 1 to 3 inches. That covers bermuda, zoysia and St. Augustine comfortably, and a fine fescue or perennial rye lawn kept at the upper end of its range. It does not reach the 3.5 inches a tall fescue lawn prefers in summer heat. The 10-inch front wheels are the underrated feature. Small wheels drop into every dip in an uneven lawn and the reel scalps the high spots; bigger wheels bridge those dips and keep the cut more even. The trade-off for the width is effort. A wider reel moves more grass per step, and in thick or damp growth you feel it through the handle. Plan on checking the bedknife gap when it arrives, as many owners find it needs a quarter-turn of adjustment before it cuts cleanly."
  },
  {
    "id": "great-states-415-16-reel-mower",
    "name": "Great States 415-16 16-Inch 5-Blade Push Reel Mower",
    "category": "16-inch, 5 Blades, 1/2 to 2-1/2 inch Cut Height",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 3000,
    "pros": [
      "Five-blade reel on a 16-inch frame is the sweet spot of cut quality and push effort",
      "Cuts as low as 1/2 inch, which suits bermuda and zoysia kept short",
      "About 25 pounds, easy to carry and store on a hook",
      "Tempered alloy steel reel and bedknife, with a welded torsion bar frame",
      "Priced in the budget tier while still using a five-blade reel"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Tops out at 2-1/2 inches, too low for tall fescue or bluegrass in summer",
      "Smaller wheels follow ground contours closely, so bumpy lawns can show scalped patches"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right default for a small warm-season lawn: light, cheap, and five blades for a cleaner cut than the 4-blade budget models.",
    "description": "If you have a small bermuda, zoysia or centipede lawn and want a reel mower without overthinking it, this is the one we would point you to first. The 16-inch width is the balance point of the category. Narrower mowers take noticeably more passes, and wider ones take more effort per step; at 16 inches with five blades, most adults can mow a 2,000 square foot lawn without a break. Five blades matter because every blade that passes the bedknife makes a cut, and more cuts per wheel rotation means grass is sheared more times per inch of travel. That gives a cleaner, more even finish, especially at low heights. The height range of 1/2 inch to 2-1/2 inches is aimed squarely at warm-season grass. Bermuda kept at an inch or so looks its best under a reel, and this one gets there easily. The limitation is the top end. Tall fescue and bluegrass want 3 inches or more in summer, and forcing them to 2-1/2 inches stresses the lawn. At about 25 pounds it is easy to hang on a garage wall. Uneven lawns will show every high spot at low settings, so level the worst dips before you commit to a short cut."
  },
  {
    "id": "american-lawn-mower-1815-18-reel-mower",
    "name": "American Lawn Mower Company 1815-18 18-Inch 5-Blade Reel Mower",
    "category": "18-inch, 5 Blades, 1/2 to 2-3/4 inch Cut Height",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Size",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.2,
    "reviewCount": 1800,
    "pros": [
      "18-inch width saves passes without the heavy push of a 20-inch reel",
      "Height range of 1/2 to 2-3/4 inches is slightly more flexible than the 16-inch models",
      "10-inch composite wheels ride better on uneven ground",
      "Heat-treated alloy steel blades on a ball-bearing reel",
      "From the longest-running reel mower maker in the US, with parts easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "At around 27 pounds it is heavier to lift than the 16-inch models",
      "Still too low at the top end for cool-season lawns in summer"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A good middle ground for a 3,000 to 4,000 square foot warm-season lawn that makes a 16-inch reel feel slow.",
    "description": "The American Lawn Mower Company has been making push reels for more than a century, and the 1815-18 is the model in its range we think most owners should look at. It splits the difference between the nimble 16-inch reels and the wide, effortful 20-inch ones. Two extra inches of width over a 16-inch mower does not sound like much, but it cuts the number of passes on a typical lawn by about a tenth, and it does so without the noticeably heavier push of a 20-inch reel. The height range runs from 1/2 inch to 2-3/4 inches. That quarter-inch more at the top than the Great States model gives a little more room for grasses like perennial ryegrass or fine fescue, and for letting warm-season grass stay taller in a drought. It still is not a cool-season lawn mower in summer. The 10-inch wheels bridge small dips better than 8-inch wheels, which reduces scalping on the kind of slightly lumpy suburban lawn most of us actually have. Parts availability is a quiet advantage. Bedknives, wheels and sharpening kits for this brand are easy to get, which matters for a tool that can last decades if maintained."
  },
  {
    "id": "american-lawn-mower-1204-14",
    "name": "American Lawn Mower Company 1204-14 14-Inch 4-Blade Reel Mower",
    "category": "14-inch, 4 Blades, 1/2 to 1-3/4 inch Cut Height",
    "badge": "Best for Tiny Lawns",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.1,
    "reviewCount": 15000,
    "pros": [
      "About 19 pounds, the lightest mower here and easy to carry up steps to a front lawn",
      "14-inch width fits narrow side strips and small townhouse lawns",
      "Among the least expensive ways to get a real scissor cut",
      "Very simple mechanism with little to go wrong",
      "Huge owner base, so adjustment tips and spare parts are easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Maximum height of 1-3/4 inches is too short for most lawns other than bermuda and zoysia",
      "Four blades and a light reel stall in thick or overgrown grass"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Only for very small, short-cut warm-season lawns, where its light weight and low price make it hard to beat.",
    "description": "This is the reel mower most people have seen, and the one behind most of the complaints about reel mowers in general. Both reputations are deserved. On a small bermuda or zoysia lawn mowed often, it is a delight: light enough to carry with one hand, narrow enough to fit a side yard, and capable of a clean scissor cut at an inch or so. At about 19 pounds it is the mower to choose when the lawn is a townhouse strip or a front yard up a flight of steps. The problems start when it is bought for the wrong lawn. The maximum cutting height is 1-3/4 inches, which is far too short for tall fescue, bluegrass or St. Augustine, and scalping those grasses to that height weakens them within a season. With four blades rather than five and a lighter reel, it also has less momentum, so grass that has got away from you bends over rather than being cut. Mow it every four to five days in the growing season and it performs. Skip a week and you will be going over the same strip twice. Buy it for the right lawn and it will last a long time; buy it because it is cheap and you will probably hate it."
  },
  {
    "id": "greenworks-20-inch-reel-mower-25072",
    "name": "Greenworks 20-Inch 5-Blade Push Reel Mower with Grass Catcher 25072",
    "category": "20-inch, 5 Blades, 1-3/8 to 2-1/2 inch Cut, Catcher",
    "badge": "Best with Grass Catcher",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4,
    "reviewCount": 1500,
    "pros": [
      "Includes a rear grass catcher, which most reels either lack or sell as an extra",
      "20-inch width with five helical blades",
      "Nine height positions from 1-3/8 to 2-1/2 inches",
      "10-inch front and 6-inch rear wheels help stop the reel scalping",
      "Three-year warranty is longer than most manual reels"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Lowest setting of 1-3/8 inches is too high for anyone wanting a golf-short bermuda",
      "At about 30 pounds with the catcher it is the heaviest of the conventional reels"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth it if you want clippings collected rather than left on the lawn, and do not need a very short cut.",
    "description": "Most reel mowers throw clippings forward or backward and leave them on the lawn, which is usually fine because reel clippings are fine and break down fast. Some owners want them collected anyway, whether for a tidy finish, for compost, or because the lawn backs onto a patio. The Greenworks 20-inch includes a rear grass catcher in the box, which is the main reason to choose it. The reel itself is a five-blade, 20-inch unit with nine height positions from 1-3/8 to 2-1/2 inches. That is a narrower window than the Scotts, and it is shifted upward: it will not go below about an inch and a third, so it is not the mower for bermuda kept golf-course short, but it suits zoysia, St. Augustine kept low, and centipede. The 10-inch front and 6-inch rear wheels are a sensible layout for keeping the reel level on uneven ground. The catcher adds weight and it fills faster than you expect on a lawn that has been left to grow, so this is best on a lawn you mow often. The three-year warranty is a small but real point in its favour in a category where one year is typical."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Reel Mowers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-reel-mower/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are reel mowers good for bermuda grass?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, bermuda is the grass reel mowers suit best. It looks its best when kept short, usually between half an inch and an inch and a half, and a reel shears it cleanly at those heights where a rotary often tears and scalps. That is why golf courses use reels on bermuda. Choose a five-blade reel that goes down to half an inch."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a reel mower cut tall fescue?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most cannot cut it well, because tall fescue should be kept at 3 to 4 inches and most reels top out at 3 inches or lower. The Fiskars StaySharp Max, which reaches 4 inches, is the exception on this list. Even then, mow often, because tall fescue that gets long tends to flop over rather than feed into the reel."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How big a lawn can I mow with a reel mower?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most adults, up to about 2,000 to 3,000 square feet is comfortable, and a flat lawn of up to around 5,000 square feet is workable if you enjoy the exercise and mow often. Beyond that the job takes long enough that people start skipping mowings, which is exactly when reel mowers perform worst."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often does a reel mower need sharpening?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Much less often than a rotary blade. Most home reels need adjusting several times a season but backlapping only once every one to three years, depending on lawn size and how much debris they hit. A reel with nicks or a rounded bedknife may need professional grinding, which a lawn equipment shop can do for a moderate fee."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do reel mowers work on bumpy lawns?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not well. The cutting cylinder sits close to the ground, so on uneven lawns it scalps the high spots and misses the dips. Larger wheels help by bridging small dips, which is why 10-inch wheels are worth having. Cutting a little higher also helps. A lawn full of roots or mole hills is better served by a rotary mower."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are powered reel mowers worth it?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Right now we would still choose a good manual reel for most home lawns. The battery-assisted reel mowers we looked at had thin review histories and repeated complaints about struggling in damp grass. Gas-powered front-throw reels are excellent but are specialist machines costing many times more, sold mainly through dealers for golf-quality bermuda and zoysia."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I bag clippings from a reel mower?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually not. Reel clippings are short and fine, and they sift down into the lawn and break down quickly, returning nitrogen to the soil. Collecting them makes sense for a very tidy finish, for composting, or if clippings would blow onto a patio or pool. If you want a catcher included, the Greenworks 20-inch comes with one."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Lawn & Yard Care",
    tags: [
      "best reel mower",
      "push reel mower",
      "manual reel mower",
      "reel mower for bermuda",
      "reel vs rotary mower",
      "backlapping reel mower"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            6 Reel Mowers Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Reel Mowers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A reel mower gives a small lawn the cleanest cut it will ever get, for no fuel and almost no noise. It also punishes the wrong grass, the wrong height and the wrong yard. Here is how to tell which side of that line you are on.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#fiskars-staysharp-max-18-reel-mower"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Is a Reel Right for You?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#right-for-you" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Is a Reel Mower Right for You?</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#grass-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Which Grasses Suit a Reel</a>
          <a href="#cutting-height" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Cutting Height Ranges</a>
          <a href="#blade-count" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Blade Count, Width and Wheels</a>
          <a href="#scissor-cut" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Scissor Cut Explained</a>
          <a href="#twigs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Twigs, Cones and Debris</a>
          <a href="#backlapping" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Backlapping and Bedknife Adjustment</a>
          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. When to Buy Something Else</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A reel mower is not a cheap rotary mower with the engine removed. It is a different cutting tool that shears grass like scissors, and on the right lawn it beats a rotary on cut quality outright. On the wrong lawn, with tall fescue grown out to four inches and a scatter of oak twigs, it is a miserable afternoon. Most bad reel mower reviews are really reviews of that mismatch.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started from the reel mowers that are actually available to buy online in the US, which is a shorter list than it looks. Many listings are the same two or three frames sold under different brand names, and several powered reel mowers have come and gone with very thin review histories. We checked every mower here against the maker's own specifications for cutting width, blade count and, above all, cutting height range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cutting height was the first filter because it decides what lawns a mower can serve at all. A mower that tops out at 1-3/4 inches can only look after bermuda or zoysia well, whatever else it does right. We then weighed blade count and reel width, which together decide cut quality and how long the job takes, and wheel size, which decides how evenly the reel follows a lumpy lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            In our testing we paid most attention to three situations where reels fail: grass that had gone a week past its mowing day, damp grass in the morning, and lawns with twigs and seed pods scattered over them. We also checked how easy each mower was to adjust, because a reel that arrives with the bedknife out of adjustment will chew instead of cut, and many owners never realise it can be fixed in five minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            We left out powered reel mowers this time. The battery-assisted models we looked at had too few owner reviews, and too many of those mentioned struggling in damp grass, to recommend over a good manual reel. Gas-powered front-throw reels of the kind used on golf greens are a different class of machine at many times the price, and they are sold through specialist dealers rather than general retailers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="right-for-you" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Is a Reel Mower Right for Your Lawn?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Answer three questions honestly before buying: how big is the lawn, what grass is it, and how often will you mow. A reel mower is excellent when the answers are small, warm-season or fine-bladed, and at least once a week. It is frustrating when any one of them goes the other way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size first. A push reel is comfortable up to around 2,000 to 3,000 square feet for most adults, and workable up to about 5,000 on a flat lawn if you enjoy the exercise. Beyond that, the job becomes long enough that people start skipping mowings, and a reel mower punishes skipped mowings more than any other kind. If your lawn is bigger than that, our guide to the <a href="/best-push-mower/">best push mowers</a> covers gas and battery options that will not leave you dreading Saturday.
          </p>

          <p>
            Grass second. Reels are at their best on grasses that are kept short and have fine, dense blades, which mainly means bermuda and zoysia. They are fine on centipede, fine fescue and perennial ryegrass. They struggle with tall fescue, bluegrass kept tall and St. Augustine allowed to get thick, because those grasses either want a height above what most reels can reach or have stems a light reel bends rather than cuts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frequency third. A reel cuts well only when grass stands up into the blades, and long grass flops over. The one-third rule, which says never remove more than a third of the blade in one mowing, matters more with a reel than with a rotary. In practice that means mowing every four to seven days in peak season, not every ten to fourteen.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under 3,000 sq ft, warm-season grass, mowed weekly: buy a reel.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your lawn fails any one of those three conditions, a reel can still work, but check the cutting height range and weight carefully. If it fails two, buy a rotary mower instead.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Terrain also matters. Reels need a reasonably flat, smooth lawn, because the cutting cylinder sits close to the ground and scalps the high spots on a lumpy one. A few dips are fine; a yard full of tree roots and mole hills is not reel territory.
          </p>
        </div>

