        <div id="grass-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Grasses Suit a Reel Mower
          </h2>

          <p>
            Grass type is the single biggest predictor of whether you will be happy with a reel mower, and it is the thing most reviews skip. The key is the height the grass wants to be kept at, compared with the height range of the mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bermuda is the ideal reel grass. It looks best cut short, often between 1/2 inch and 1-1/2 inches, and it grows sideways into a dense mat that a reel shears cleanly. Golf courses and sports fields mow bermuda with reels for exactly this reason: a rotary at those heights tends to tear and scalp, while a reel leaves a uniform, carpet-like surface. Zoysia is similar, doing well at 1 to 2 inches, although its stiff blades demand a sharp, well-adjusted reel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Centipede and St. Augustine are workable. Centipede at 1 to 2 inches suits most reels. St. Augustine is kept taller, often 2-1/2 to 4 inches depending on variety and shade, so only the mowers with higher ranges can serve it, and its thick stolons can stall light reels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tall fescue is where most reel mower disappointment comes from. It should be mowed at around 3 to 4 inches, especially in summer heat, and most reel mowers stop at 2-1/2 or 3 inches. Cutting it shorter to suit the mower thins the lawn and invites weeds and heat stress. Its coarse, upright blades also tend to push aside rather than feed into the reel once they get long. Kentucky bluegrass is similar at 2-1/2 to 3-1/2 inches. If your lawn is one of these, the Fiskars StaySharp Max with its 4-inch top setting is the only mower on this list we would recommend.
          </p>

