          <p>
            Not sure what grass you have? Look at a blade closely. Fine, narrow blades that spread by runners and turn tan in winter are almost always bermuda or zoysia. Wide, coarse blades that grow in clumps and stay green in cool weather are almost always tall fescue.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cutting-height" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cutting Height: The Spec That Decides Everything
          </h2>

          <p>
            When comparing reel mowers, look at the maximum cutting height before anything else. It varies from 1-3/4 inches on the smallest budget reels to 4 inches on the Fiskars, and it decides which lawns a mower can look after at all. The minimum height matters too, but mainly to bermuda owners chasing a golf-course finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Height on a reel mower is set by raising or lowering the rear roller or wheels relative to the cutting cylinder. On cheaper mowers that means adjusting a bolt or bracket on each side, and you need to set both sides equally or the cut will be slanted. Better mowers use a single lever or a handle-mounted adjuster. Whatever the mechanism, measure the result with a ruler from a hard surface to the bedknife edge, because the markings on the mower are not always accurate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that the setting is not the same as the result on a soft lawn. The wheels sink slightly into turf and thatch, so a mower set at 2 inches may leave grass at about 1-3/4 inches. On a thatchy zoysia lawn the difference can be larger. Set a little higher than your target, mow a strip, and measure the grass itself.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">HEIGHT RANGES ON THIS LIST</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fiskars StaySharp Max maximum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Scotts Classic 20-inch maximum</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/2 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Lowest setting on the Great States and ALM reels</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1-3/4 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">ALM 1204-14 maximum</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pick a height based on your grass, not the mower, and then pick the mower that reaches that height with room to spare. You want your normal setting in the middle of the mower's range, not at the very top, so you can raise it in drought or heat. A bermuda lawn mowed at an inch works with any mower here; a fescue lawn mowed at 3-1/2 inches works with one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blade-count" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Blade Count, Reel Width and Wheels
          </h2>

          <p>
            Blade count is the most visible spec on a reel mower and the most misunderstood. More blades mean more cuts per revolution of the reel, which means grass is clipped more often for each inch the mower travels. That gives a finer, more even finish, and it matters most at low heights, where each blade has to catch short grass cleanly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Four blades is the budget standard and fine for a lawn kept at a medium height and mowed often. Five blades is the sweet spot for most home lawns and is what we would look for by default. Seven or more blades are found on golf and specialist machines and are aimed at very short bermuda; on a home lawn kept above an inch, the extra blades add little except cost and push effort.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reel width decides how long the job takes. A 14-inch reel on a 2,000 square foot lawn needs roughly 40 percent more passes than a 20-inch reel. Wider reels also need more force to push, because they are cutting more grass with every step. For most people, 16 to 18 inches is the right balance; 20 inches suits bigger lawns and stronger pushers, and 14 inches suits tiny lawns and narrow strips.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wheel size is the spec people ignore and then regret. Small 8-inch wheels drop into every dip, which tips the reel and scalps the high ground on either side. Larger 10-inch wheels bridge those dips and keep the cylinder level. On a perfectly smooth lawn it makes little difference; on a normal suburban lawn it is the difference between an even finish and a striped, patchy one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weight cuts both ways. A heavier reel has more momentum and cuts through thick patches more reliably, which is why the 52-pound Fiskars handles overgrown grass better than a 19-pound budget reel. But weight has to be pushed uphill and carried up steps. If your lawn has a real slope or needs carrying to reach, the lighter models are easier to live with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="scissor-cut" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Scissor Cut and Why It Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            A rotary mower cuts grass by hitting it with a blade spinning at high speed. Even a sharp rotary blade is really striking the grass at a speed that snaps it off, and a dull one tears it. A reel mower works differently. As the blades on the spinning cylinder pass a fixed bar called the bedknife, they trap each grass blade and shear it, exactly as scissors do.
          </p>

          <p>
            That shearing action leaves a clean, flat cut rather than a frayed tip. A clean cut loses less moisture, heals faster and is less open to disease, and it does not turn brown at the tips a day or two after mowing, which is the tell-tale sign of a dull rotary blade. On fine-bladed grasses kept short, the difference is visible from across the street.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clippings are the other benefit. Reel clippings are short and fine, and they sift down into the lawn and break down quickly rather than sitting on top in clumps. For most lawns there is no need to collect them, and leaving them returns nitrogen to the soil.
          </p>

          <p>
            The scissor cut depends entirely on the gap between the reel blades and the bedknife. If it is too wide, the blades push grass over instead of trapping it, leaving uncut blades standing up in a ragged line. The standard test is a strip of paper: slide it between the reel and bedknife and turn the reel by hand. A properly adjusted reel cuts the paper cleanly along the full width. If it folds or tears the paper, it needs adjusting.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also why a rotary mower's blade condition matters so much by comparison. If you own both kinds, our guide on <a href="/how-often-to-sharpen-mower-blades/">how often to sharpen mower blades</a> covers the rotary side, where a dull blade quietly costs you lawn health all season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="twigs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Twigs, Pine Cones and Other Reel-Killers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A rotary mower smashes through debris. A reel mower stops dead. A twig thicker than a pencil, a pine cone, a sweetgum ball or a walnut will jam between the reel and the bedknife, stop the mower with a jolt, and sometimes nick the edge or knock the bedknife out of adjustment.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes a quick walk over the lawn before mowing part of the job. On a lawn under deciduous trees in autumn, or under pines, sweetgums or oaks at any time of year, you will be picking up debris every time. Some people find that becomes a relaxing habit; others find it the reason they give up on the reel. Be honest about which you are before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tall seed heads and weeds are the other problem. Wiry stems, like those of plantain and dandelion flowers or the seed stalks of bahiagrass, often bend under the reel and spring back up uncut. Bahiagrass is so bad for this that most owners of it should not consider a reel at all. On other lawns, a quick pass with a string trimmer over the worst seed heads solves it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HABIT THAT SAVES A REEL</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Walk the lawn first. Every time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Two minutes picking up sticks and cones before mowing prevents most jams, most nicked blades and most bedknife problems. If your yard is under trees that drop debris all season, factor that time in or choose a rotary.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Damp grass is a milder version of the same problem. Wet grass lies down and clumps, and a reel cuts it poorly. Mow a reel lawn in the afternoon, once the dew has dried off, and you will see a real difference in cut quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="backlapping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Backlapping and Bedknife Adjustment
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reel mowers need sharpening far less often than rotary blades, because the self-sharpening contact between reel and bedknife keeps the edges honed. But they do need occasional attention, and knowing how to do it yourself is the difference between a reel that cuts well for decades and one that gets thrown out after three seasons.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adjustment comes first and is needed far more often. On most manual reels there are two adjustment screws or bolts, one on each side, that move the bedknife closer to or further from the reel. Use the paper test described above, working from one side to the other, and adjust until the reel cuts paper cleanly across its full width with only light contact. Too tight and the mower is hard to push and wears fast; too loose and it chews grass instead of cutting it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Backlapping is the home sharpening method for reels. You coat the reel blades with a lapping compound, a grit paste, and turn the reel backwards against the bedknife, either by hand with a crank or by propping the mower and turning the wheels backwards. The grit hones both edges together so they meet cleanly. Most brands sell a sharpening kit that includes the compound and a handle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once a year is plenty for most home lawns, and many owners need it only every two or three seasons. If the reel has a nick from hitting a stone, or the bedknife is visibly rounded, backlapping may not be enough and a professional grind is the answer. A lawn equipment shop with reel-grinding equipment can restore a mower that seems worn out.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Fiskars StaySharp Max is the exception. Its cutting system is designed so the blades do not contact the bedknife, and Fiskars pitches it as avoiding annual sharpening. That does not mean never sharpening, but it does mean less routine upkeep than a conventional reel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When to Buy Something Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            We like reel mowers, but they are the right tool for fewer lawns than their fans suggest. If your lawn is bigger than about 5,000 square feet, a reel will take too long for most people to keep up with. If it is tall fescue or bluegrass, only one reel on this list can mow it at a healthy height. If it is under trees, you will spend as much time picking up sticks as mowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            For small lawns that do not suit a reel, a compact battery rotary is usually the best alternative. They are nearly as quiet, need no fuel, cut at any height, and deal with twigs and long grass without fuss. Our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/">best lawn mowers for small yards</a> compares reels, battery mowers and corded mowers side by side for exactly this situation.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what appeals to you is mainly the quiet and the lack of fuel and oil, the <a href="/best-electric-lawn-mower/">best electric lawn mowers</a> give you most of that on a bigger lawn, with far less effort. They also bag, mulch and cope with a missed week, which a reel does poorly.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you are keeping a rotary as your main mower, the finish it gives depends largely on the blade. A fresh, sharp blade of the right type makes more difference than most people expect; see our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-blades/">best lawn mower blades</a> for how to match and choose one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a reel for tall fescue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most reel mowers top out between 1-3/4 and 3 inches, and tall fescue wants 3 to 4 inches. Forcing it lower thins the lawn, invites weeds and stresses the grass in summer heat. Unless you buy a reel that reaches 4 inches, a rotary is the better tool for a fescue lawn.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a week of mowing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A reel cuts grass that stands up into the blades. Once grass gets long it bends over and the reel rolls across it, leaving a ragged, uneven finish and uncut stalks. Mow every four to seven days in the growing season, and never take more than a third of the blade in one pass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never adjusting the bedknife</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many reel mowers arrive with the bedknife slightly out of adjustment, and many owners decide the mower is bad without checking. Use the paper test: the reel should cut a strip of paper cleanly across its full width. A five-minute adjustment fixes most complaints about chewed or missed grass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing over sticks and cones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twigs, pine cones and seed pods jam the reel and can nick the blades or knock the bedknife out of line. A quick walk over the lawn before mowing prevents most problems. Under trees that drop debris all season, factor in that time or choose a rotary mower instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing wet grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Dew and rain make grass lie flat and clump, and a reel cuts it poorly. It also leaves clippings clumped on the surface instead of sifting down into the lawn. Wait until the afternoon, when the grass has dried and stands up, for a noticeably cleaner cut and an easier push.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are reel mowers good for bermuda grass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, bermuda is the grass reel mowers suit best. It looks its best when kept short, usually between half an inch and an inch and a half, and a reel shears it cleanly at those heights where a rotary often tears and scalps. That is why golf courses use reels on bermuda. Choose a five-blade reel that goes down to half an inch.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a reel mower cut tall fescue?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most cannot cut it well, because tall fescue should be kept at 3 to 4 inches and most reels top out at 3 inches or lower. The Fiskars StaySharp Max, which reaches 4 inches, is the exception on this list. Even then, mow often, because tall fescue that gets long tends to flop over rather than feed into the reel.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a lawn can I mow with a reel mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most adults, up to about 2,000 to 3,000 square feet is comfortable, and a flat lawn of up to around 5,000 square feet is workable if you enjoy the exercise and mow often. Beyond that the job takes long enough that people start skipping mowings, which is exactly when reel mowers perform worst.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often does a reel mower need sharpening?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Much less often than a rotary blade. Most home reels need adjusting several times a season but backlapping only once every one to three years, depending on lawn size and how much debris they hit. A reel with nicks or a rounded bedknife may need professional grinding, which a lawn equipment shop can do for a moderate fee.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do reel mowers work on bumpy lawns?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not well. The cutting cylinder sits close to the ground, so on uneven lawns it scalps the high spots and misses the dips. Larger wheels help by bridging small dips, which is why 10-inch wheels are worth having. Cutting a little higher also helps. A lawn full of roots or mole hills is better served by a rotary mower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are powered reel mowers worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Right now we would still choose a good manual reel for most home lawns. The battery-assisted reel mowers we looked at had thin review histories and repeated complaints about struggling in damp grass. Gas-powered front-throw reels are excellent but are specialist machines costing many times more, sold mainly through dealers for golf-quality bermuda and zoysia.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I bag clippings from a reel mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Reel clippings are short and fine, and they sift down into the lawn and break down quickly, returning nitrogen to the soil. Collecting them makes sense for a very tidy finish, for composting, or if clippings would blow onto a patio or pool. If you want a catcher included, the Greenworks 20-inch comes with one.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

